// The shape of `barista::customer_impl::emit_does_pre_and_post`, with
// everything but the shape taken away.
//
// A function guarded by a precondition and answering a postcondition is
// *constantly true* when you ask "either the precondition failed, or the answer
// satisfies the postcondition" — that is what a contract means, and it is a
// claim about the code rather than about any value it runs on. The barista's
// version of that claim is buried under four states, a tuple of three and an
// event enum. This one has two symbols and a pair, and states the same thing.

symbol t1
symbol t2

// The precondition: the input is one of the two symbols we know.
#[total]
function is_tag {
    pick 0
    push t1
    equal
    branch {
        drop 0
        push true
    } {
        push t2
        equal
    }
}

// The postcondition: the answer is one of the two pairs we make.
#[total]
function is_numbered {
    pick 0
    push t1
    push 1
    tuple 2
    equal
    branch {
        drop 0
        push true
    } {
        push t2
        push 2
        tuple 2
        equal
    }
}

// The function under contract: `t1` numbers as 1, `t2` as 2, and anything else
// gets the empty tuple — which is exactly the answer the postcondition rejects,
// and exactly the input the precondition rules out.
#[precondition(is_tag)]
#[postcondition(is_numbered)]
#[total]
function number {
    pick 0
    push t1
    equal
    branch {
        drop 0
        push t1
        push 1
        tuple 2
    } {
        pick 0
        push t2
        equal
        branch {
            drop 0
            push t2
            push 2
            tuple 2
        } {
            drop 0
            tuple 0
        }
    }
}

// `not precondition or (function ; postcondition)`, written the way a branch
// writes it. Nothing about this reads as constant — it copies its input, tests
// it, and on one path runs the whole of `number` and checks the answer.
#[total]
function number_does_pre_and_post {
    pick 0
    jump is_tag
    branch {
        jump number
        jump is_numbered
    } {
        drop 0
        push true
    }
}

// The claim, and it is not proved yet. Two things stand between the term and
// `drop 0 push true`, and only one of them has been dealt with.
//
// **Identifying the two tests.** `is_tag` asks `v == t1` and `number` asks it
// again; nothing is decidable until the rewriter knows those are the same
// question. `copy_nat` is the law that says so and `share { … }` places it, but
// its window wants `pick 0 ; X ; dip m { X }` adjacent — so `number`'s copy has
// to be lifted out of the arm (`lift`), sunk past the branch (`sink`), and
// split out of the frame it lands in (`inv(fuse)`, `fuse`) first. Eight aimed
// steps, all of them existing laws.
//
// **Splitting the boolean.** With one test shared, `split_bool` puts a literal
// where the boolean was — and used to stall holding `pick 0 ; is_bool`, a
// question the equation set could answer and no matcher could read. That gap is
// closed: see `identities::the_guard_a_split_leaves`.
//
// What is left is the third instance of the same obstacle. `v == t2` is
// computed under a frame, so it is not on top, and every law about *what a
// value is* is stated about the top of the stack. Bringing it up is `unframe`,
// which is an equation the set already has and which nothing can place. So this
// stays a comment until there is a matcher for it.
//
// `probes::number_pre_and_post` is the address to work it at.
//
// identity number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant
//     { jump number_does_pre_and_post }
//   = { drop 0 push true };
